<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Reference alert emitter for mulesoft-autoscaler.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  ===============
  This autoscaler was originally designed to be driven by Anypoint Monitoring
  alerts with webhook actions. That capability does not exist. Anypoint has three
  alerting systems - Monitoring custom-dashboard alerts, Runtime Manager alerts,
  and API Manager alerts - and all three deliver by email only, at every
  subscription tier including Titanium. There is no HTTP destination anywhere.

  So nothing MuleSoft ships is going to POST to /autoscale/webhook. Something you
  control has to. That is what this file is: a drop-in flow for the application
  you want scaled, which measures itself and reports to the autoscaler.

  Because the caller is now yours rather than MuleSoft's, the request shape is
  defined by this project rather than reverse-engineered from a vendor. See the
  "Inbound contract" section of README.md.

  HOW TO USE
  ==========
  1. Copy this file into the MONITORED application's src/main/mule/ directory -
     not into the autoscaler itself. It measures the app it runs inside.
  2. Add one line to each flow whose traffic should count towards scaling:

         <flow-ref name="autoscaler-count-request"/>

     Put it at the start of the flow, after the listener.
  3. Add the properties listed below to that application's configuration.
  4. Redeploy the monitored application.

  The autoscaler needs no change to accept this: the body below already matches
  the shape normalize-alert reads.

  SAFETY
  ======
  The error handler at the bottom swallows everything. This flow runs inside your
  application, and a monitoring sidecar that can take down the thing it monitors
  is worse than no monitoring at all. If the autoscaler is unreachable, the wrong
  secret is configured, or the endpoint 500s, this logs a warning and the host
  application carries on undisturbed.

  WHY REQUEST RATE, AND NOT CPU OR HEAP
  =====================================
  Because a Mule application cannot portably observe its own machine metrics, and
  pretending otherwise produces a fragile example. Both obvious routes were tried
  and both failed on a current runtime:

    - java.lang.Runtime via DataWeave's java! interop returns null. DataWeave's
      dot notation on a Java object performs JavaBean getter access, not
      arbitrary method invocation, so totalMemory() resolves as a missing key
      rather than a call.

    - ManagementFactory::getMemoryMXBean().heapMemoryUsage resolves the getter
      correctly but then dies on the Java 17 module system:
      "class org.mule.weave.v2.module.pojo.reader.PropertyDefinition cannot
      access class sun.management.MemoryImpl because module java.management does
      not export sun.management". DataWeave reflects on the implementation class,
      which is encapsulated.

  CPU is no better: getSystemLoadAverage() reports the host rather than the
  container, so on CloudHub 2.0 it reflects noisy neighbours instead of this
  replica, and getProcessCpuLoad() requires a non-portable com.sun.management
  cast.

  Request rate needs no reflection, no added dependency, and no JVM flags, so it
  survives runtime and Java upgrades. It is also the more useful signal: scaling
  on an application-level measure is the one thing this autoscaler can do that
  native CloudHub autoscaling cannot.

  Substituting your own signal means editing one transform below - the autoscaler
  acts on currentValue and ignores what produced it. Queue depth, in-flight
  count, or orders-per-minute all work the same way.

  ACCURACY
  ========
  The counter is a non-atomic read-modify-write, so under concurrency it
  undercounts slightly. That is deliberate: a scaling signal needs to be roughly
  right and cheap, and locking a shared store on every inbound request would cost
  more than the imprecision does. Do not reuse this counter for billing.

  REQUIRED PROPERTIES
  ===================
    autoscaler.host                  host of the deployed autoscaler, no scheme
                                     e.g. mulesoft-autoscaler-abc123.region.cloudhub.io
    autoscaler.secret                must equal the autoscaler's webhook.secret
    autoscaler.emit.interval.seconds measurement window. 60 is reasonable; keep
                                     it well below the autoscaler's cooldown
    autoscaler.up.threshold          requests/minute at or above which to report
    autoscaler.down.threshold        requests/minute at or below which to report
    autoscaler.deployment.id         THIS app's CloudHub 2.0 deployment id
    autoscaler.application.name      label used in the autoscaler's logs
    autoscaler.organization.id       Anypoint organization id
    autoscaler.environment.id        Anypoint environment id

  A CloudHub 2.0 application has no way to discover its own deployment id at
  runtime, so autoscaler.deployment.id must be supplied. Find it by running
  "anypoint-cli-v4 runtime-mgr application list" against your org and
  environment; README.md carries the full command with its flags.

  Verified against Mule 4.11.6 and 4.12.2.
-->
<mule xmlns="http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/core"
      xmlns:doc="http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/documentation"
      xmlns:ee="http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/ee/core"
      xmlns:http="http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/http"
      xmlns:os="http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/os"
      xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      xsi:schemaLocation="
        http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/core http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/core/current/mule.xsd
        http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/ee/core http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/ee/core/current/mule-ee.xsd
        http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/http http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/http/current/mule-http.xsd
        http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/os http://www.mulesoft.org/schema/mule/os/current/mule-os.xsd">

    <http:request-config name="Autoscaler_Request_config" doc:name="Autoscaler endpoint">
        <http:request-connection protocol="HTTPS" host="${autoscaler.host}" port="443"/>
    </http:request-config>

    <!-- In-memory on purpose: this holds a count for the current window only.
         Losing it on restart costs one window of signal, and a restarted app has
         no traffic history worth preserving anyway. -->
    <os:object-store name="Autoscaler_Metrics"
                     doc:name="Request counter"
                     persistent="false"
                     maxEntries="10"/>

    <!--
      Call this from each flow whose traffic should count towards scaling:
          <flow-ref name="autoscaler-count-request"/>
    -->
    <sub-flow name="autoscaler-count-request">
        <os:retrieve key="requests" objectStore="Autoscaler_Metrics" target="requestCount" doc:name="Read counter">
            <os:default-value>#[0]</os:default-value>
        </os:retrieve>
        <os:store key="requests" objectStore="Autoscaler_Metrics" doc:name="Increment counter">
            <os:value>#[(vars.requestCount default 0) + 1]</os:value>
        </os:store>
    </sub-flow>

    <flow name="autoscaler-metrics-emitter">
        <scheduler doc:name="Close the measurement window">
            <scheduling-strategy>
                <fixed-frequency frequency="${autoscaler.emit.interval.seconds}"
                                 startDelay="${autoscaler.emit.interval.seconds}"
                                 timeUnit="SECONDS"/>
            </scheduling-strategy>
        </scheduler>

        <os:retrieve key="requests" objectStore="Autoscaler_Metrics" target="windowCount" doc:name="Read window count">
            <os:default-value>#[0]</os:default-value>
        </os:retrieve>

        <!-- Reset immediately so the next window starts clean even if the report
             below fails. A dropped report should cost one window, not corrupt
             every window after it. -->
        <os:store key="requests" objectStore="Autoscaler_Metrics" doc:name="Reset counter">
            <os:value>#[0]</os:value>
        </os:store>

        <ee:transform doc:name="Compute requests per minute">
            <ee:variables>
                <ee:set-variable variableName="ratePerMinute"><![CDATA[%dw 2.0
output application/java
var count   = (vars.windowCount default 0) as Number
var seconds = p('autoscaler.emit.interval.seconds') as Number
---
if (seconds <= 0) 0 else (count * 60) / seconds
]]></ee:set-variable>
            </ee:variables>
        </ee:transform>

        <!-- Report only when the value is actionable. The autoscaler would ignore
             an in-band value anyway, so sending it is a round trip that can only
             fail; staying quiet is strictly better. -->
        <choice doc:name="Worth reporting?">
            <when expression="#[vars.ratePerMinute >= (p('autoscaler.up.threshold') as Number) or vars.ratePerMinute &lt;= (p('autoscaler.down.threshold') as Number)]">
                <http:request method="POST"
                              config-ref="Autoscaler_Request_config"
                              path="/autoscale/webhook"
                              doc:name="Report to autoscaler">
                    <http:body><![CDATA[#[output application/json
---
{
    alert: {
        id: "emitter-" ++ (now() as String {format: "yyyyMMddHHmmss"}),
        metric: "requests-per-minute",
        currentValue: vars.ratePerMinute,
        resource: {
            id: p('autoscaler.deployment.id'),
            name: p('autoscaler.application.name'),
            environment: { id: p('autoscaler.environment.id') }
        },
        organization: { id: p('autoscaler.organization.id') }
    }
}]]]></http:body>
                    <http:headers><![CDATA[#[output application/java
---
{
    "X-Autoscaler-Secret": p('autoscaler.secret'),
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
}
]]]></http:headers>
                </http:request>

                <logger level="INFO" doc:name="Log report"
                        message="#['Reported ' ++ (vars.ratePerMinute as String) ++ ' req/min to the autoscaler; it answered ' ++ (payload.status default 'no status')]"/>
            </when>
        </choice>

        <error-handler>
            <!-- Never let monitoring break the thing it monitors. -->
            <on-error-continue type="ANY" logException="false" doc:name="Emitter failure is not the app's problem">
                <logger level="WARN" doc:name="Log emitter failure"
                        message="#['Could not report metrics to the autoscaler: ' ++ (error.description default 'unknown error') ++ '. The application is unaffected.']"/>
            </on-error-continue>
        </error-handler>
    </flow>
</mule>
